1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,226
Ravi thinks a manager just gives orders.

2
00:00:03,526 --> 00:00:05,212
But look at one busy day.

3
00:00:05,761 --> 00:00:07,737
He welcomes the school principal.

4
00:00:07,987 --> 00:00:10,449
He hears that milk prices will rise.

5
00:00:10,699 --> 00:00:13,136
He calms a customer who got cold tea.

6
00:00:13,386 --> 00:00:15,250
And he bargains with the milk seller.

7
00:00:15,850 --> 00:00:18,225
A manager does not do one job.

8
00:00:18,225 --> 00:00:19,801
He plays ten roles.

9
00:00:20,501 --> 00:00:22,601
So, what are managerial roles?

10
00:00:22,901 --> 00:00:27,199
Roles are the different parts a
manager plays at work, like an actor.

11
00:00:27,799 --> 00:00:32,835
In 1973, Henry Mintzberg
watched real managers at work.

12
00:00:33,135 --> 00:00:37,427
In his book, The Nature of
Managerial Work, he found ten roles.

13
00:00:37,727 --> 00:00:39,980
And he sorted them into three groups.

14
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:41,430
Group one.

15
00:00:41,417 --> 00:00:44,538
Interpersonal roles,
meaning roles with people.

16
00:00:44,838 --> 00:00:45,738
Figurehead.

17
00:00:45,698 --> 00:00:49,350
Ravi greets the principal,
as the face of Chai Corner.

18
00:00:49,650 --> 00:00:50,550
Leader.

19
00:00:50,333 --> 00:00:53,576
He guides and motivates
Asha, Kabir and Meena.

20
00:00:53,876 --> 00:00:54,776
Liaison.

21
00:00:54,616 --> 00:00:58,201
He builds links outside,
like the bakery next door.

22
00:00:58,751 --> 00:00:59,693
Group two.

23
00:00:59,693 --> 00:01:03,569
Informational roles, meaning
roles with news and facts.

24
00:01:03,869 --> 00:01:04,769
Monitor.

25
00:01:04,603 --> 00:01:07,157
He hears that milk prices will rise.

26
00:01:07,457 --> 00:01:08,442
Disseminator.

27
00:01:08,442 --> 00:01:10,771
He passes that news to his team.

28
00:01:11,071 --> 00:01:12,076
Spokesperson.

29
00:01:12,076 --> 00:01:15,142
He speaks for Chai
Corner to people outside.

30
00:01:15,692 --> 00:01:16,717
Group three.

31
00:01:16,717 --> 00:01:20,118
Decisional roles, meaning
roles where he decides.

32
00:01:20,418 --> 00:01:21,387
Entrepreneur.

33
00:01:21,387 --> 00:01:24,233
He tries something new, like ginger chai.

34
00:01:24,533 --> 00:01:25,844
Disturbance handler.

35
00:01:25,844 --> 00:01:28,208
He calms the customer with cold tea.

36
00:01:28,508 --> 00:01:29,808
Resource allocator.

37
00:01:29,808 --> 00:01:32,899
He decides how to use
people, time and money.

38
00:01:33,199 --> 00:01:34,099
Negotiator.

39
00:01:34,044 --> 00:01:36,265
He bargains with the milk seller.

40
00:01:36,915 --> 00:01:39,107
Roles are what a manager does.

41
00:01:39,107 --> 00:01:41,404
Skills are what a manager needs.

42
00:01:41,804 --> 00:01:46,978
In 1955, Robert Katz named three
skills every manager needs.

43
00:01:47,328 --> 00:01:48,403
Technical skill.

44
00:01:48,403 --> 00:01:51,714
Knowing how to do the work,
like making perfect chai.

45
00:01:52,014 --> 00:01:52,914
Human skill.

46
00:01:52,888 --> 00:01:56,121
Working well with people,
like keeping the team happy.

47
00:01:56,421 --> 00:01:57,553
Conceptual skill.

48
00:01:57,553 --> 00:02:00,837
Seeing the big picture, like
planning a second stall.

49
00:02:01,437 --> 00:02:03,456
But the mix changes with the level.

50
00:02:03,756 --> 00:02:07,067
Lower level managers use
technical skill the most.

51
00:02:07,367 --> 00:02:10,369
Top managers use
conceptual skill the most.

52
00:02:10,669 --> 00:02:13,006
And human skill matters at every level.

53
00:02:13,656 --> 00:02:14,590
Quick check.

54
00:02:14,590 --> 00:02:17,373
Ravi tells his team a big order is coming.

55
00:02:17,373 --> 00:02:18,760
Which role is this?

56
00:02:20,660 --> 00:02:21,704
Disseminator!

57
00:02:21,704 --> 00:02:24,284
He passes useful news to his team.

58
00:02:24,884 --> 00:02:25,893
So, remember.

59
00:02:26,193 --> 00:02:30,704
Mintzberg found ten roles in three
groups: interpersonal, informational

60
00:02:30,704 --> 00:02:31,868
and decisional.

61
00:02:32,218 --> 00:02:35,958
Katz named three skills:
technical, human and conceptual.

62
00:02:36,308 --> 00:02:41,228
Top managers need the most conceptual
skill, and everyone needs human skill.

63
00:02:41,828 --> 00:02:45,280
Next time, we'll trace the
evolution of management thought.
